ines.  \n\n   *Step D – assembling the configuration.*  \n   The \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (non‑sunny) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines from Step C give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n5. **Small case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Direct constructions show that each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable:\n   * \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\);\n   * \\(k=1\\): two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and one sunny line \\(y=-x+4\\);\n   * \\(k=2\\): one vertical line \\(x=1\\) and two sunny lines obtained from the Lemma for \\(k=2\\);\n   * \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines given by the Lemma for \\(k=3\\).\n\n   Hence all four values occur.\n\nCombining steps 2–5 we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
