            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\]\n\nthe diagonal lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Basic properties of a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and let \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) with\n\\((a_1,b_1)\\neq(a_2,b_2)\\).  \nIf \\(a_1=a_2\\) then \\(\\ell\\) would be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), contradiction; similarly \\(b_1=b_2\\) would make \\(\\ell\\) horizontal.  \nIf \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\) then \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nHence on a sunny line the three quantities \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(a+b\\) are pairwise distinct for distinct points of the line.\n\nConsequently a sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that all of them are sunny.  \nBecause each \\(\\ell_i\\) meets each diagonal at most once, the total number of incidences\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\\ell_i\\cap S_n|\n\\]\nis at most the number of ordered pairs \\((i,c)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^2\\).  \nBut we know the exact total number of points:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\\ell_i\\cap S_n|=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the average number of points per line equals \\(\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\).\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) is not an integer, contradicting the fact that each \\(|\\ell_i\\cap S_n|\\) is an integer.  \nTherefore **for even \\(n\\) at most \\(n-1\\) of the lines can be sunny**.\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd the average \\(\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) is an integer, so the parity argument does not forbid all \\(n\\) lines being sunny. We now show that this is indeed possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for odd \\(n\\) (all \\(n\\) lines sunny)\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is odd; then \\(2\\) is invertible modulo \\(n\\).  \nFor each residue \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+2y\\equiv c\\pmod n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (1) can be written as \\(x+2y=c+tn\\) with some integer \\(t\\).  \nAll solutions of (1) lie on a line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (which is sunny); different residues \\(c\\) give distinct parallel lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3.1  \nFor every diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) and every residue \\(c\\) there exists **exactly one** point of \\(S_n\\) belonging to both \\(L_c\\) and \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx+y=s,\\\\\nx+2y\\equiv c\\pmod n\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nis equivalent to  \n\n\\[\ny\\equiv c-s\\pmod n,\\qquad x=s-y .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le y\\le s-1\\) (the points of \\(D_s\\) inside \\(S_n\\) have precisely those \\(y\\)-coordinates), the congruence \\(y\\equiv c-s\\pmod n\\) has exactly one solution in this interval: the map\n\\(y\\mapsto y\\pmod n\\) is a bijection from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,s-1\\}\\) onto a set of size \\(s-1\\le n\\), and \\(c-s\\) determines a unique residue class. Hence a unique integer \\(y\\) with \\(1\\le y\\le s-1\\) satisfies the congruence, and then \\(x=s-y\\) also lies in \\([1,n]\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nBy Lemma 3.1 each diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains exactly one point of each line \\(L_c\\). Since there are \\(n\\) diagonals and \\(n\\) residues, the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=0}^{n-1}\\) together contain all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(S_n\\). All lines have slope \\(-\\frac12\\), hence are sunny, and they are distinct because they correspond to different residues \\(c\\).  \n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for even \\(n\\) (exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even. Apply the construction of Section 3 to the odd integer \\(n-1\\).  \nWe obtain \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=0}^{n-2}\\) that cover all points with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n\nThe remaining points are those on the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\).  \nAdd this diagonal line (which is non‑sunny) to the family.  \n\nNow we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_n\\); exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  \nStart from the configuration of Section 3 (all \\(n\\) lines sunny).  \nFor any residue \\(c\\) replace the sunny line \\(L_c\\) by the non‑sunny diagonal \\(D_{c+2}\\) (the diagonal that contains precisely the same points of \\(S_n\\) as \\(L_c\\) does).  \nEach replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \nConsequently we obtain configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  \nBegin with the configuration of Section 4 (exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines).  \nAgain, replace any of the sunny lines by its corresponding diagonal \\(D_{c+2}\\).  \nEach replacement lowers \\(k\\) by one, so we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the impossibility result of Section 2 with the explicit constructions of Sections 3–5, we have completely determined the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus the answer announced in the summary is proved. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
