tisfies (6), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_c\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies\non exactly one of the lines \\(L_c\\).\n\n*Intersection with diagonals.*  Let \\(D_s\\) be a diagonal (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\nIf \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) and \\(a+2b\\equiv a+2(s-a)=2s-a\\pmod n\\); as\n\\(a\\) runs through the \\(s-1\\) admissible values, the residues\n\\(2s-a\\) are all distinct.  Hence each diagonal meets every line \\(L_c\\) in at\nmost one point (in fact in exactly one point when \\(s-1\\le n\\)).  Consequently\nthe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration with **\\(k=n\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for even \\(n\\) (exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) be even.  Apply the construction of Section 3 to the odd integer\n\\(n-1=2m-1\\).  This yields \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=0}^{n-2}\\) that\ncover all points with \\(a+b\\le n\\) (i.e. the set \\(S_{n-1}\\)).  The points with\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) are precisely the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  Adding this diagonal,\nwhich is non‑sunny, we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole set\n\\(S_n\\); exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.  Hence the bound (5) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\n#### Odd \\(n\\).\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose any set \\(C\\subset\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\) and keep the sunny\nlines \\(\\{L_c\\mid c\\in C\\}\\).  \n\nFor a diagonal \\(D_s\\) we have \\(|D_s|=s-1\\).  \nIf \\(s-1\\le k\\) then the \\(k\\) chosen sunny lines already meet \\(D_s\\) in\n\\(s-1\\) distinct points, so \\(D_s\\) is completely covered.  \nIf \\(s-1>k\\) we add the diagonal line \\(D_s\\) itself to the family.\nThe diagonals that need to be added are exactly those with\n\\(s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\)