                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in \\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor an integer \\(c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1)\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb R^{2}\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\]\n\nthe “diagonal’’ lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Basic properties of a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and let \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in\\ell\\cap S_{n}\\) with\n\\((a_{1},b_{1})\\neq(a_{2},b_{2})\\).  \n\n*If \\(a_{1}=a_{2}\\) then \\(\\ell\\) would be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\));\n*if \\(b_{1}=b_{2}\\) then \\(\\ell\\) would be horizontal (slope \\(0\\));\n*if \\(a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}\\) then \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nHence on a sunny line the three numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(a+b\\) are pairwise distinct for distinct points.\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\tag{1}\n\\text{a sunny line meets each diagonal }D_{c}\\text{ in at most one point.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even  \n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) covering all points of\n\\(S_{n}\\) and that all of them are sunny.\nBecause of (1), each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) can be intersected by at most one point of each line.\nSince the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, every line must contain **exactly**\none point of \\(D_{n+1}\\); otherwise some point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) would remain uncovered.\n\nWrite the points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) as \\((i,n+1-i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\).\nThus line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and therefore uses row \\(i\\) and column\n\\(n+1-i\\).\n\nConsider now the diagonal \\(D_{n}\\), which consists of the points\n\\((i,n-i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\).\nBecause of (1) each line can contain at most one of these points.\nHence we have to assign the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\) to the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) in such a way that\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}\\text{ does not contain a point with row }i\\text{ or column }n+1-i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCondition (2) is exactly the statement that the bipartite graph with left part\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (the rows of \\(D_{n}\\)) and right part\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (the columns of \\(D_{n}\\)) has a perfect matching that avoids the\nforbidden edges \\((i,i)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\).\nWhen \\(n\\) is even the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) can be split into the two halves\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\tfrac n2\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\tfrac n2+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\nFor every \\(i\\le \\tfrac n2\\) the two forbidden columns are \\(i\\) and \\(n+1-i\\);\nthe latter belongs to the opposite half.\nConsequently the first half is adjacent only to the second half, so Hall’s condition\nfails for the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\tfrac n2\\}\\).\nThus no assignment satisfying (2) exists, and the assumption that all\n\\(n\\) lines are sunny is impossible.\n\nHence for **even** \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{at most }n-1\\text{ of the }n\\text{ lines can be sunny}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A construction with \\(n\\) sunny lines for odd \\(n\\)\n\nNow let \\(n\\) be **odd**.\nFor each residue \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we define a line\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+2y=C_{c},\n\\qquad\\text{where }C_{c}\\in\\{c,\\;c+n\\}\n\\text{ is the unique integer belonging to the interval }[\\,s+1,\\,2s-1\\,]\n\\text{ for every }s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Why does such a \\(C_{c}\\) exist?*  \nFix a diagonal \\(D_{s}\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\).\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in D_{s}\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) and therefore\n\n\\[\na+2b = a+2(s-a)=2s-a .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(a\\) runs through the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\) the quantity\n\\(2s-a\\) runs through the interval  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,2s-1,\\,2s-2,\\dots ,s+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe length of this interval is \\(s-2\\le n-1\\); because \\(n\\) is odd,\nthe interval contains **exactly one** integer congruent to each residue\n\\(c\\pmod n\\).  Hence for every residue \\(c\\) there is a unique integer\n\\(C_{c}\\) belonging to (5).  This integer is either \\(c\\) or \\(c+n\\); we\nchoose it as in (4).  Consequently, for the fixed diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) the\nset \\(L_{c}\\cap D_{s}\\) consists of a single point, namely the point whose\ncoordinates satisfy \\(a+2b=C_{c}\\).\n\nBecause the choice of \\(C_{c}\\) does not depend on \\(s\\), the line \\(L_{c}\\)\nintersects **every** diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) in exactly one point.\nAll the lines \\(L_{c}\\;(c=0,\\dots ,n-1)\\) are distinct (they have different\nconstants \\(C_{c}\\)) and have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), which is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{c}\\) is sunny.\n\nFinally, the union of the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{c}\\}_{c=0}^{n-1}\\) contains, for\nevery diagonal \\(D_{s}\\), exactly one point of each diagonal; hence it\ncontains all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\nThus for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines for even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even.\nApply the construction of Section 3 to the odd integer \\(n-1\\).\nThis yields \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points with\n\\(a+b\\le n\\) (i.e. the set \\(S_{n-1}\\)).\nThe remaining points of \\(S_{n}\\) are precisely those of the diagonal\n\\(D_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\).\nAdding this diagonal (which is non‑sunny) gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all of \\(S_{n}\\); exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.\nHence the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Obtaining every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nIn the construction of Section 3 each diagonal \\(D_{s}\\;(s\\le n)\\) meets **every**\nsunny line \\(L_{c}\\) in exactly one point, while the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) meets\n\\(L_{c}\\) in the unique point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) where \\(i\\) is the row of that\npoint.  Consequently, for any \\(c\\) the line \\(L_{c}\\) contains exactly one\npoint of the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\nPick a sunny line \\(L_{c}\\) and replace it by the diagonal \\(D_{c'}\\) that\ncontains its unique point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) (the diagonal is non‑sunny).\nAll other points of \\(L_{c}\\) lie on diagonals \\(D_{s}\\;(s\\le n)\\); each such\ndiagonal is still intersected by all the remaining sunny lines, so those\npoints remain covered.  Thus the covering property is preserved while the\nnumber of sunny lines is reduced by one.\n\nStarting from the maximal configuration (Section 3 for odd \\(n\\), Section 4\nfor even \\(n\\)) and performing the above replacement repeatedly, we obtain\nconfigurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, where\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe parity argument of Section 2 shows that for even \\(n\\) at most \\(n-1\\) of\nthe \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, and the construction of Section 4 shows that\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines are indeed attainable.  \nFor odd \\(n\\) the construction of Section 3 provides a configuration with\nall \\(n\\) lines sunny, and the reduction described in Section 5 yields all\nsmaller values of \\(k\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\] \n\nThis completes the proof. ∎"
