ause the points of \\(D_{c}\\) are all distinct and each belongs to\na different line, we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{c}=|D_{c}\\cap S_{n}|=c-1\\qquad(2\\le c\\le n+1). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nIn particular \\(t_{2}=1\\); thus there is exactly one line, call it\n\\(\\ell_{1}\\), that meets the diagonal \\(D_{2}\\).  The only point of \\(D_{2}\\) is\n\\((1,1)\\), so \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains \\((1,1)\\).\n\nA sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) must intersect the next diagonal\n\\(D_{3}\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)) at a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=3\\).\nThe only possibilities are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); both share either the\n\\(x\\)-coordinate or the \\(y\\)-coordinate with \\((1,1)\\), which would make the\nline horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) – all forbidden.\nHence no sunny line can contain \\((1,1)\\) and also a point of \\(D_{3}\\).\n\nBut (2.1) with \\(c=3\\) says that the diagonal \\(D_{3}\\) must be intersected by\ntwo distinct lines, one of which is \\(\\ell_{1}\\).  This is impossible.\nTherefore a covering of \\(S_{n}\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines cannot exist\nwhen \\(n\\) is even.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{for even }n\\text{ at most }n-1\\text{ of the lines can be sunny}.}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction with \\(n\\) sunny lines for odd \\(n\\)\n\nAssume now that \\(n\\) is odd.  Then the integer \\(2\\) is invertible modulo\n\\(n\\).  For each residue  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+2y\\equiv c\\pmod n . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(x+2y=c+tn\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\) shows that (3.1) is a straight line of\nslope \\(-\\tfrac12\\); this slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every\n\\(L_{c}\\) is sunny.  Distinct residues give distinct parallel lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 3.2.**  \nThe family \\(\\{L_{c}\\}_{c=0}^{n-1}\\) partitions the set \\(S_{n}\\); i.e. each\n\\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_{c}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a given point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) the integer \\(a+2b\\) has a unique residue\n\\(c\\) modulo \\(n\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-1\\); by definition \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n\\(x+2y\\equiv c\\pmod n\\), so \\((a,b)\\in L_{c}\\).  If \\((a,b)\\) belonged to two\ndifferent lines \\(L_{c}\\) and \\(L_{c'}\\) we would have\n\\(a+2b\\equiv c\\equiv c'\\pmod n\\), forcing \\(c=c'\\).  ∎\n\nBecause the lines partition \\(S_{n}\\), each line contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|S_{n}|}{n}= \\frac{n(n+1)/2}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  In particular every line meets each diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) in **at most**\none point, and the points on a line have pairwise distinct rows, columns and\ndiagonals, as required for a sunny line.\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration consisting of the \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines \\(\\{L_{c}\\}_{c=0}^{n-1}\\); this yields a covering with  \n\n\\[\nk=n\\quad\\text{sunny lines}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines for even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even and put \\(m=n-1\\) (so \\(m\\) is odd).  \nApply the construction of Section 3 to the integer \\(m\\); we obtain\n\\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_{c}\\}_{c=0}^{m-1}\\) that cover all points of  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le m+1=n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points of \\(S_{n}\\) are precisely those on the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAdding this diagonal (which is non‑sunny) to the family gives \\(m+1=n\\) distinct\nlines covering all of \\(S_{n}\\), and exactly \\(m=n-1\\) of them are sunny.\nHence the bound (2.2) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Obtaining every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nThe two constructions above give the **maximal** possible numbers of sunny\nlines: \\(n\\) for odd \\(n\\) and \\(n-1\\) for even \\(n\\).\nWe now show how to reduce the number of sunny lines while keeping a covering\nwith exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nLet a configuration contain a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) and a diagonal \\(D_{s}\\).\nBecause a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point, the diagonal\n\\(D_{s}\\) contains exactly one point of \\(\\ell\\).  Moreover, the other\n\\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_{s}\\) lie on \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines (different from\n\\(\\ell\\)).  If we **replace all those \\(s-1\\) sunny lines by the single diagonal\n\\(D_{s}\\)**, the covering property is preserved and the number of sunny lines\ndecreases by \\(s-1\\).\n\nThe numbers \\(s-1\\) range over \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the diagonals are\n\\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\)).  Starting from a configuration with the\nmaximal number of sunny lines we may perform the above replacement for a\ndiagonal \\(D_{r+1}\\); this reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly \\(r\\).\nHence for odd \\(n\\) we obtain configurations with any prescribed  \n\n\\[\nk=n-r\\qquad(0\\le r\\le n),\n\\]\n\ni.e. with every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nFor even \\(n\\) we start from the configuration of Section 4, which already has\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Replacing the sunny lines intersecting a diagonal\n\\(D_{r+1}\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\)) reduces the number of sunny lines by \\(r\\) and\nkeeps the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  Consequently we obtain\nconfigurations with every  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-1)-r\\qquad(0\\le r\\le n-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. with every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility result (Section 2) with the explicit constructions\n(Sections 3–5) we have completely determined the possible numbers of sunny\nlines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus the answer announced in the summary is proved. ∎"
